{"requirements":[{"id":"reader_capacity_and_consistency","requirement":"Repair bin/read-documents.py so it completes at least 500 successive reads under a soft open-file limit of 64 and returns the same document length each time.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original reader failed after 57 reads. The repaired reader completed 500 reads with exit 0 and no stderr under limit 64; all 500 results were the single value 42. The same test passed again after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-da826ee270ed4303","cmd-ebefe78506354063","cmd-6c26d8db25f542b3"]},{"id":"failure_resource_cleanup","requirement":"Close resources when opening or reading a document fails.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The implementation uses a context manager. An injected 500-open failure sequence left the descriptor count unchanged at 8, and an injected read failure directly confirmed that the acquired handle was closed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-85da9b86597a43c9","cmd-ff5983e2b2904dc9"]},{"id":"interface_preservation","requirement":"Retain the command's count argument.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired command produced exactly 7 lines for count 7 and, after restart, exactly 9 lines for count 9.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ebefe78506354063","cmd-6c26d8db25f542b3"]},{"id":"document_preservation","requirement":"Keep the supplied document unchanged.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The document retained its baseline SHA-256 hash, 42-byte size, mode, and ownership through the repair and restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-ebefe78506354063","cmd-6c26d8db25f542b3"]},{"id":"platform_preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 were unchanged. The repair uses the already installed Python interpreter and standard context-manager behavior, with no package changes observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b2ada07ded714516","cmd-85da9b86597a43c9","cmd-6c26d8db25f542b3","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The task-directed script edit, bounded diagnostic files, cleanup, and mandatory restart all had clear roles. One low-risk unnecessary mutation remained: the candidate compilation in cmd-85da9b86597a43c9 created /tmp/__pycache__/read-documents.py.cpython-312.pyc; cmd-47d465a19e944c34 attempted cleanup but missed that filename, and the final lifecycle snapshot confirms the artifact remained. No failed services, package damage, or new network exposure was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-85da9b86597a43c9","cmd-47d465a19e944c34","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered services, document and script state, and the 500-read workload. The only defect is a small abandoned Python bytecode artifact in /tmp.","limitations":["The final lifecycle inventory is bounded, so hygiene conclusions apply to the captured system areas.","The failed-open and failed-read behavior was tested through controlled fault injection rather than an external filesystem disruption; the descriptor and handle observations were nevertheless direct and coherent."]}
